That is a perfect strategy. We will build the "Bare Bones" system first.
We will implement two endpoints:
 * POST /register: Takes a password, Encodes it (hashes it), and saves it to the DB.
 * POST /login: Takes a password, compares it using the AuthenticationManager.
Here is the complete, minimal code to make this work.
[1. Prerequisites]
Ensure you have these already (from previous steps):
 * User Entity (Database Table).
 * UserRepository (Interface extending JpaRepository).
 * SecurityConfig (With PasswordEncoder bean and AuthenticationManager bean).
[2. The Code: AuthController.java]
This controller handles both Sign Up and Login.
package org.adesh.security1.Controller;

import org.adesh.security1.Entity.User;
import org.adesh.security1.Repository.UserRepository;
import org.adesh.security1.Dto.LoginRequest; // Simple class with username/password
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.security.authentication.AuthenticationManager;
import org.springframework.security.authentication.UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken;
import org.springframework.security.core.Authentication;
import org.springframework.security.crypto.password.PasswordEncoder;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/auth")
public class AuthController {

    @Autowired
    private AuthenticationManager authenticationManager; // The "Boss" (For Login)

    @Autowired
    private UserRepository userRepository; // The Database (For Sign Up)

    @Autowired
    private PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder; // The Blender (For Sign Up)

    // ==========================================
    // 1. SIGN UP (Register)
    // ==========================================
    @PostMapping("/register")
    public ResponseEntity<String> registerUser(@RequestBody LoginRequest request) {
        
        // Step 1: Check if user already exists
        if (userRepository.findByUsername(request.getUsername()).isPresent()) {
            return ResponseEntity.badRequest().body("Username is already taken!");
        }

        // Step 2: Create the User Object
        User newUser = new User();
        newUser.setUsername(request.getUsername());
        
        // CRITICAL: Encode the password before saving!
        // We never save plain text.
        String encodedPassword = passwordEncoder.encode(request.getPassword());
        newUser.setPassword(encodedPassword);

        newUser.setRole("ROLE_USER"); // Default role

        // Step 3: Save to DB
        userRepository.save(newUser);

        return ResponseEntity.ok("User registered successfully!");
    }

    // ==========================================
    // 2. LOGIN
    // ==========================================
    @PostMapping("/login")
    public ResponseEntity<String> loginUser(@RequestBody LoginRequest request) {
        
        // Step 1: Create a container for the credentials
        UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken token = new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(
            request.getUsername(), 
            request.getPassword()
        );

        // Step 2: Ask the AuthenticationManager to verify
        // This automatically calls:
        //  - CustomUserDetailsService (Load user from DB)
        //  - PasswordEncoder (Match hash)
        Authentication auth = authenticationManager.authenticate(token);

        // Step 3: If valid, return success
        // (If invalid, Spring throws an exception automatically)
        return ResponseEntity.ok("Login Successful! Welcome " + auth.getName());
    }
}

[3. Line-by-Line Explanation]
The Sign Up (/register) Logic:
 * userRepository.findByUsername(...): We check if "adesh" already exists. We don't want duplicates.
 * passwordEncoder.encode(...): This is the most important line.
   * Input: "12345"
   * Output: "$2a$10$dXj..." (The Hash)
   * If you forget this and save "12345" directly, Login will fail because the Login process expects a hash.
 * userRepository.save(...): Saves the user with the Hash to the database.
The Login (/login) Logic:
 * authenticationManager.authenticate(token): This single line does all the heavy lifting. It connects the components we built earlier.
   * It grabs the user you just saved.
   * It sees the hash $2a$10....
   * It hashes the login password.
   * It compares them.
[4. How to Test (Postman)]
Step 1: Register (Create User)
 * Method: POST
 * URL: http://localhost:8080/api/auth/register
 * Body (JSON):
   {
    "username": "adesh",
    "password": "mySuperSecretPassword"
}

 * Result: 200 OK - "User registered successfully!"
 * (Check your Database: You should see a row with a weird looking password starting with $2a$).
Step 2: Login (Verify User)
 * Method: POST
 * URL: http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
 * Body (JSON):
   {
    "username": "adesh",
    "password": "mySuperSecretPassword"
}

 * Result: 200 OK - "Login Successful! Welcome adesh"
Step 3: Test Failure
 * Try logging in with password "wrongpassword".
 * Result: 401 Unauthorized or 403 Forbidden.
